# Core Rules

## Code Quality

- Write code for the next reader, not the next compiler — clarity over cleverness.
- One function, one responsibility. If you need "and" to describe what it does, split it.
- Prefer flat over nested. Early returns, guard clauses, and simple conditions reduce cognitive load.
- Delete dead code immediately; version control is the safety net.
- Avoid magic numbers and strings — name every constant that isn't self-evident.
- Keep functions short enough to fit on one screen without scrolling.
- Consistent naming beats clever naming; follow the existing conventions of the codebase.

## Testing

- Write tests before or alongside code, never as an afterthought.
- Each test must verify one behavior and have a descriptive name that reads as a specification.
- Tests must be deterministic — no reliance on system time, random values, or execution order.
- Prefer integration tests over unit tests for behavior that crosses system boundaries.
- Do not mock internal code; only mock at the boundary of external systems.
- A failing test suite blocks all merges, no exceptions.
- Aim for coverage of behaviors, not coverage of lines.

## Security

- Treat all external input as untrusted; validate and sanitize at every entry point.
- Never hardcode secrets, credentials, or keys — use environment variables or a secrets manager.
- Apply the principle of least privilege to services, roles, and API keys.
- Sanitize all output rendered to users to prevent injection attacks.
- Keep dependencies up to date; automate vulnerability scanning in CI.
- Log security-relevant events (auth failures, permission denials) but never log credentials or PII.
- Default to deny; explicitly grant permissions rather than explicitly denying them.

## Error Handling

- Handle errors at the layer that has enough context to act on them; propagate upward otherwise.
- Never silently swallow exceptions — log or surface every failure.
- Distinguish between recoverable errors (retry, fallback) and unrecoverable ones (fail fast).
- Return structured error types, not raw strings, so callers can act programmatically.
- Provide actionable error messages: what failed, why, and what to do next.

## Version Control

- Each commit represents one logical change; never bundle unrelated modifications.
- Write commit messages in imperative mood: "Add X", "Fix Y", "Remove Z".
- The commit body explains *why*, not what — the diff already shows what changed.
- Branch from the main branch; keep branches short-lived (days, not weeks).
- Never force-push to a shared branch without team consensus.
- All changes enter the main branch via a reviewed pull request.

## Documentation

- Document the *why* and the *constraints*, not the *what* — code explains itself.
- Keep documentation co-located with code so it stays current.
- README must cover: purpose, local setup, and how to run tests.
- Mark all public APIs with intent and contract; omit implementation details.
- Delete documentation that is no longer accurate; stale docs are worse than none.

## Dependencies

- Add a dependency only when the benefit clearly outweighs the maintenance cost.
- Pin exact versions in application code; use compatible ranges only in library code.
- Audit new dependencies for security posture, license, and maintenance activity.
- Remove unused dependencies before merging.
- Isolate third-party integrations behind an abstraction layer so they can be swapped.

## Configuration & Environments

- Separate configuration from code; the same binary must run in every environment.
- Define a single source of truth for configuration schema with validation on startup.
- Provide safe defaults — the system should start and degrade gracefully without optional config.
- Never commit environment-specific values; use templated examples (`.env.example`).

## Observability

- Emit structured logs (key-value or JSON) for machine parseability.
- Log at the right level: DEBUG for development detail, INFO for operational events, ERROR for failures requiring attention.
- Every external call must be traceable — include correlation IDs across service boundaries.
- Expose health-check and readiness endpoints for all long-running services.
- Define and track SLIs before a service goes to production.

## Performance

- Measure before optimizing; never guess at bottlenecks.
- Cache at the layer closest to the consumer, but define clear invalidation rules.
- Paginate or stream large result sets; never return unbounded collections.
- Avoid N+1 query patterns by fetching related data in bulk.
- Set timeouts on every network call and external dependency.

## Code Review

- Review for correctness, security, and maintainability — not style (automate style).
- Approve only code you understand well enough to maintain yourself.
- Comments must be specific and actionable; avoid vague feedback.
- Authors address or explicitly defer every review comment before merging.
- Reviewer is a collaborator, not a gatekeeper — suggest, don't dictate.

## Analysis Before Implementation

- For any bug fix, refactor, new feature, performance improvement, dependency change, or config schema change, share an RCA brief before writing code. When multiple approaches have meaningfully different trade-offs, present ranked options with a recommendation; otherwise commit to one.
- The brief must state: the root cause (mechanism, not symptom), the proposed approach (what changes and what does not), every file that will be modified or created, and risks or trade-offs.
- Approval is explicit ("yes", "go ahead", "looks good", "implement it") or implicit (the user re-states the same request after reading the brief). Silence, follow-up questions, and option-selection refinement ("what about X?") are not approval to implement.
- For single-file, self-evident changes, a one-sentence statement of intent and rationale satisfies the brief requirement.
- Does not apply to typo fixes, trivial renames, formatting, or doc-only changes.
- Do not implement first and explain after. Do not list hypotheses without committing to one. Do not use vague scope ("several files") — enumerate exactly which files.

