# Architecture Patterns

## Separation of Concerns

- Divide systems into layers with clear responsibilities: presentation, business logic, data access.
- No layer may skip a layer below it or depend on a layer above it.
- Business logic must not leak into the presentation or persistence layer.

## Domain-Driven Design (DDD) Essentials

- Model software around the business domain, using the same language as domain experts (ubiquitous language).
- Group code by domain concepts (bounded contexts), not technical roles (controllers/services/repos).
- Protect domain invariants inside aggregates; only the aggregate root exposes mutation.
- Use domain events to communicate state changes across bounded contexts.

## Hexagonal Architecture (Ports & Adapters)

- The core domain has zero knowledge of infrastructure — databases, queues, HTTP frameworks.
- Define ports (interfaces) in the domain; implement adapters in the infrastructure layer.
- Swap any adapter (e.g., database, message broker) without touching business logic.
- All dependencies point inward toward the domain, never outward.

## Service Design

- Design services around business capabilities, not data entities.
- Each service owns its data store; no direct cross-service database access.
- Communicate synchronously (REST/gRPC) for queries that need immediate response; asynchronously (events/queues) for state changes.
- Services must be independently deployable and independently scalable.
- Design for failure: every service-to-service call may fail and must degrade gracefully.

## API Design

- APIs are contracts; treat breaking changes with the same gravity as data migrations.
- Version APIs from day one (`/v1/…`); never break an existing published version.
- Use consistent resource naming, status codes, and error envelope formats across all endpoints.
- Validate request payloads at the API boundary, not deep in business logic.
- Design APIs around intent (what the caller wants to accomplish), not around internal data models.

## Event-Driven Architecture

- Prefer events for cross-bounded-context communication to keep services decoupled.
- Events are immutable facts in the past tense: `OrderPlaced`, `PaymentFailed`.
- Design consumers to be idempotent — the same event may be delivered more than once.
- Store events before publishing (outbox pattern) to prevent lost messages on failure.
- Define and version event schemas explicitly; treat schema changes as breaking changes.

## Data Management

- Each service is the single authoritative source for its domain data.
- Denormalize read models (CQRS read side) rather than writing complex queries against the write model.
- Prefer append-only event logs over mutable state where auditability matters.
- Apply database migrations as versioned, forward-only scripts; never edit a committed migration.
- Plan for eventual consistency in distributed systems; document where it applies.

## Resilience Patterns

- Apply circuit breakers around all remote calls to prevent cascade failures.
- Use retries with exponential back-off and jitter; set a maximum retry budget.
- Implement bulkheads to isolate failures — a slow dependency must not exhaust shared resources.
- Prefer timeouts over waiting indefinitely; fail fast and let the caller decide how to recover.
- Test failure modes in lower environments (chaos engineering) before they hit production.

## Security Architecture

- Authenticate at the entry point of every service; never trust a caller because of network position.
- Issue short-lived tokens; rotate credentials automatically.
- Encrypt data at rest and in transit; treat plaintext storage of sensitive data as a bug.
- Isolate sensitive workloads in separate services or execution environments.
- Apply defense in depth — no single control should be the only safeguard.

## Scalability & Deployment

- Design services to be stateless; persist all shared state in external stores.
- Make deployments incremental: feature flags, blue-green, or canary releases over big-bang cut-overs.
- Automate all environment provisioning; no snowflake servers.
- Store all infrastructure definitions as code under version control.
- Build once, deploy everywhere — the same artifact must move through every environment unchanged.